{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1384104-v5-attacking-and-dodging-in-the-same-turn","id":1384104,"title":"V5 - Attacking and Dodging in the same turn","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-04-26T22:04:46-07:00","id":1384104,"title":"V5 - Attacking and Dodging in the same turn","content":"There's something that I've been confused about regarding combat, and it has to do with fighting multiple combatants. I get that if one character wants to attack multiple people they have to split their attacking pool - a PC with a Str+Brawl pool of 7 could fight two SPCs by using 4 dice on one guy and 3 dice on the other. A character defending against multiple people has a decreasing pool for dodging - a PC with a Dex+Athletics pool of 5 would roll 5 dice to dodge the first guy, 4 to dodge a second, 3 to dodge a third, etc.<br /> <br /> My question is: When a single combatant wants to attack one person solely, do they get to dodge anyone else coming at them? For instance a PC with a Str+Brawl pool of 7 and a Dex+Athletics pool of 5 is up against three guys. Could he use all 7 Brawl dice against one guy, dodge the second guy with 5 dice, and dodge the third (the second dodge) with 4 dice?<br /> <br /> I had originally thought no, because the combat rules read &quot;At the start of every turn of the conflict, each player declares their intent,&quot; &quot;Both the attacker and defender roll their pools simultaneously in a basic conflict,&quot; and &quot;If both participants are able to cause harm to their opponent, the conflict is two-sided, with both sides counting as attackers. In this case, the actions of both parties are merged into a single conflict roll.&quot; These three things together lead me to believe that you choose to do ONE thing during your turn: Attack or Defend. If you Attack, you are an Attacker, and would have to split your dice pool up to deal with multiple opponents. If you Defend, you are a Defender, and are dodging with increasing penalties to your pool. Because you declare your intent at the beginning of the turn you are only one of these two things, Attacker or Defender, and because the conflict resolves with a single simultaneous dice roll you do not get the chance to roll to dodge if you used that single conflict roll to attack.<br /> <br /> I came to doubt this upon reading <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Hmck6JnWhYZCkhcwphL5sUIoAvrpi9_wlsm0u3OIDJU/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">this errata/clarification compilation</a> which describes someone doing exactly that - going in for an attack against one target, and then dodging against the other attackers, using their attacking pool on their one attack and their dodge pool on the dodges. Is that right? That seems to run counter to the &quot;declare and then proceed&quot; guide to combat, since you wouldn't know that you needed to dodge before you were attacked. If anyone has experience with this I could use some clarification.<br /> <br /> <br /> Also while we're on the subject of combat rules, there's something else I've never understood. Page 300 describes a meatier Initiative system, by which you have an Initiative Rating equal to your Composure + Awareness, and you do not make an Initiative test. What exactly does that mean? You don't roll dice to determine Initiative, so anyone with a higher Composure+Awareness pool is <i>always </i>going to go before someone with a lower pool? That seems weird to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25972-illithidactivity","name":"IllithidActivity","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25972_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-27T02:52:35-07:00","id":1384135,"title":"","content":"Don\u2019t look at it as dodging so much, though that is the word used in the errata. Dodging implies action and though you can actively dodge it is not always accurate if you are trying to compare this style of combat to other games. It is somewhat like requiems combat were you always got a defense pool against other peoples attacks rather than 20th were you only got defense if you split or declared it so.<br /> <br /> The general thing is you may take action once per turn and if that action is against multiple people you must split your pool. This action could be attack or if out of cover actively dodging a firearm attack. <br /> <br /> The second thing is you may always use Dex+athletics as a default against anyone for any reason except against firearms when you have no cover. This takes the degradations for multiple attackers and cover if against ranged. If you are against a firearm with no cover then you can take Dex+ athletics but only if you actively dodge. Cover is important in range combat.<br /> <br /> Third thing when using Dex+athletics you can not damage that opponent. It is a one sided conflict not two.<br /> <br /> This is my understanding of how it works.<br /> <br /> In any case I would not suggest using any initiative beside the (melee, ranged, new melee, everything else) one.<br /> <br /> The reason is it complicates things more than needed and puts you in the mindset that this combat style is like other games which it is not. It is much easier to just go around the table for declarations then resolve the actions using that guideline since it all is happening somewhat simultaneously anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11720-redwulfe","name":"Redwulfe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11720_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"570"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-27T09:48:59-07:00","id":1384206,"title":"","content":"You always have a 'passive defense' even if you don't declare one; if you and James attack each other and then Peter, Madeleine and Geralt attack you, you get to decide how to react to the attacks; it's not like prior editions where your declaration 'locks' your action and you have to 'abort' to do something else.<br /> <br /> So if you wanted to really hit James and then dodge the rest, you'd do your conflict with James, then roll your deprecating defense pool (defense -1 against Peter, -2 against Maddy and -3 against Geralt). You are never denied your defense unless something specifically says you are. If you already declared you were going to stab James but James suddenly has a bunch of extra oomph, you can swap to just fully dodging James (so you'd roll defense against everyone) or you could even decide to split your attack pool against everyone (hoping to do damage to all) and see what happens. The only thing is, if you do something that changes your action (like deciding to hit James back instead of open the safe) then that will use up your action still. So you couldn't stab James to try to fend him off, AND open the safe.<br /> <br /> Also remember that V5's combat isn't explicitly one sword slash or one pull of the trigger, it's narrative and typically represents an exchange of blows or gunfire. <br /> <br /> I wrote up an extensive combat primer for V5 after discussing the (really shitty) examples of combat from the book with Karim. You can find it here: <a href=\"https://www.v5homebrew.com/wiki/Combat_Primer\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.v5homebrew.com/wiki/Combat_Primer</a>. I'm in the process of building some extra combat examples to outline some things like using Blocks and Maneuvers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1808,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","user":"elmerg","date":". 04-27-2020, 12:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-27T10:12:32-07:00","id":1384211,"title":"","content":"Huh, I really appreciate that writeup because it clarifies a lot. I'm surprised, the way the book is written seems very much to run against what the designer is saying the official rule is. It seems now bizarre to ever declare a Dodge OR splitting up your attack as opposed to attacking one person and passively dodging the others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25972-illithidactivity","name":"IllithidActivity","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25972_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-27T10:24:00-07:00","id":1384216,"title":"","content":"Yea I can see the splitting thing. But since many SPCs don't have a lot of heath splitting will probably be more prevalent when you are attacking multiple smaller combatants with large dice and non halved or aggravated damage. Especially in the upcoming Werewolf.<br /> <br /> As far as the dodge thing goes I still use it on occasion when I am caught out of cover and am closing to combat. If caught out of cover you are static difficulty 2 so it is sometimes better to get eh dice pool if you have more than 4 dice in Dex+Athletics. if not then it is a wash and better to do a different action.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11720-redwulfe","name":"Redwulfe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11720_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"570"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-27T15:00:40-07:00","id":1384275,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>IllithidActivity</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1384104-v5-attacking-and-dodging-in-the-same-turn?p=1384211#post1384211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh, I really appreciate that writeup because it clarifies a lot. I'm surprised, the way the book is written seems very much to run against what the designer is saying the official rule is. It seems now bizarre to ever declare a Dodge OR splitting up your attack as opposed to attacking one person and passively dodging the others.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Splitting is useful for some factors like if you're a strong combatant against two weak ones (say you have a 9 pool and each person you're dealing with has a 4, so you have some even numbers there) or when games use the Simplified Antagonists rule (if I have a pool of 9 and three 3 DIfficulty Antagonists, I'm going to split my pool and do cool kung fu shit narratively while still having a good chance of overcoming the antagonists all at once). <br /> <br /> Largely, though, the above is the reason why the 'declare action' phase is more about the narrative than it is about hard, brass tacks of 'this is the only thing I'm doing'. V5 focuses a lot more on narrative and narrative options for conflict. \u200bAnd as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11720-redwulfe\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11720_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11720\" data-vbnamecard=\"11720\">Redwulfe</a> says, the 'dodge' is good when you're caught in ways that would really require it, like being caught downfield of a lot of firearms, and that's also assuming you have cover you can work with too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}